**Manuscript text (plate-normalized IC50 results, uncorrected p-values; updated November 16, 2026, visually assessed bad fits removed):**

Six of 441 fitted dose-response curves (five evolved-strain curves and one ancestor curve, each from a single plate) were excluded after visual assessment showed that the fitted four-parameter logistic curve did not describe the data. This was confirmed by a group-blinded, randomized review of all curves. Where an ancestor curve was excluded, evolved strains on that plate were not normalized for that drug. All 36 populations retained at least one plate-normalized observation per drug.

After plate normalization, both evolved groups showed modestly elevated fluconazole tolerance relative to the ancestor, whereas neither group differed from the ancestor in caspofungin or amphotericin B tolerance, and the two groups did not differ from each other for any drug (Figure 2G). For fluconazole, IC~50~ was 1.39-fold higher than the ancestor in the 35°C-evolved group (one-sample *t*-test on plate-normalized log~2~[IC~50~/ancestor IC~50~], *n* = 18 populations, *p* = 0.021, 95% CI for fold change: [1.06, 1.82]) and 1.37-fold higher in the 40°C-evolved group (*n* = 18, *p* = 0.013, 95% CI: [1.08, 1.74]); the two groups were nearly identical (40°C/35°C: 0.99-fold; Welch *t*-test, *n* = 18 per group, *p* = 0.934, 95% CI: [0.70, 1.40]). Neither group differed from the ancestor in caspofungin tolerance (35°C-evolved: 1.06-fold, *p* = 0.394, 95% CI: [0.92, 1.21]; 40°C-evolved: 0.97-fold, *p* = 0.625, 95% CI: [0.84, 1.11]), and the two groups did not differ from each other (40°C/35°C: 0.91-fold; *p* = 0.341, 95% CI: [0.76, 1.10]). Neither group showed a change in amphotericin B tolerance relative to the ancestor (35°C-evolved: 1.06-fold, *p* = 0.327, 95% CI: [0.94, 1.18]; 40°C-evolved: 0.94-fold, *p* = 0.449, 95% CI: [0.78, 1.12]) or relative to each other (40°C/35°C: 0.89-fold; *p* = 0.241, 95% CI: [0.72, 1.09]). The fluconazole results remained significant after Holm correction within drug (35°C-evolved: *p* = 0.043; 40°C-evolved: *p* = 0.039) but not after Holm correction across all nine tests (*p* = 0.170 and 0.116, respectively; Table SX); we therefore interpret them as suggestive evidence of a modest increase in fluconazole tolerance shared by both selection regimes, rather than a temperature-specific effect.

