**Manuscript text (PCA results; updated November 16, 2026, visually assessed bad fits removed):**

The first two PCs together explained 60.6% of within-group variance. PC1 (32.5%) was characterized by co-loading of T~h~ (loading = −0.543), fluconazole tolerance (−0.537), amphotericin B tolerance (−0.464), and T~opt~ (−0.430) in the same direction, with caspofungin tolerance (−0.126) and CT~max~ (−0.012) contributing little. PC2 (28.2%) captured an independent axis of TPC shape variation, with CT~max~ (0.704) and T~opt~ (−0.592) loading in opposing directions; amphotericin B tolerance also loaded moderately on this axis (0.334), whereas caspofungin tolerance, fluconazole tolerance, and T~h~ loaded weakly (|loading| ≤ 0.18). The co-loading of T~h~ with fluconazole and amphotericin B tolerance on PC1 indicates a shared axis of within-group variation spanning thermal deactivation and drug tolerance, although this axis partly reflects the correlation between the two drug tolerances themselves (within-group *r* = 0.54); pairwise associations between T~h~ and fluconazole (*r* = 0.29) or amphotericin B (*r* = 0.24) tolerance were weaker. This co-loading was robust to removing the two most influential strains, individually or together (loadings of T~h~, fluconazole, and amphotericin B between −0.31 and −0.54 in all cases), whereas the contribution of T~opt~ to PC1 depended on a single strain with extreme thermal traits. Notably, fluconazole and amphotericin B both target ergosterol — fluconazole by inhibiting its synthesis and amphotericin B by binding it directly — while caspofungin acts on a mechanistically distinct target, cell wall β-glucan synthesis, and did not consistently contribute to this axis. The co-variation of T~h~ with tolerance to the two ergosterol-targeting drugs is consistent with, though not direct evidence for, a shared dependence on membrane ergosterol composition across thermal adaptation and membrane-active antifungal tolerance.

